{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310519,"nonce":"EaY/BUeb5S+m4BnEsNvwfrt5484=","title":"An abstract's property (get,set) is unreachable — ~8 conformance cases. Getter half diagnosed and made to work; setter needs value ...","message":"**`public var prop(get,set)` on an abstract is unreachable.** `a.prop` gives\n`[E0100] Cannot access field 'prop': class not registered or field does not\nexist`. **Class properties work perfectly** — that control passes, so the\nproperty machinery exists and abstracts simply never reach it.\n\nWorth ~8 conformance cases (Issue3042, Issue3067, Issue3554, Issue7883,\nIssue8337, Issue9060, Issue9290, Issue9746). Repro: `scratchpad/absprop`,\noracle `read=1 / afterset=5 / aftercompound=7`.\n\n\n`ir/tast_to_hir.rs` ~911 builds `HirAbstractField { getter: None, setter: None }`\nwith the comment *\"Will be resolved during type checking\"* — a pass that does not\nexist. TAST **does** carry the info: abstracts call the shared\n`lower_field_with_symbol`, which sets `property_access` (`decls/fields.rs:332`).\nIt is dropped at the HIR boundary. `HirAbstractField` also carries no\n`symbol_id`, so MIR cannot key the map even if it wanted to.\n\n`register_abstract_metadata` (`ir/mir/decl/metadata.rs`) never looks at\n`abstract_decl.fields` at all — only from/to and forward rules.\n\n\nCarrying `symbol_id` + `property_access` through `HirAbstractField` and\nregistering into `property_access_map` **makes the getter work** (`read=1`\ncorrect, E0100 gone). Then:\n\n1. **The setter silently drops writes** — `afterset=1`, should be 5. This is\n   semantics, not plumbing. `mir/field/lvalue.rs` ~149 calls the setter and\n   DISCARDS its result. That is right for a class (the object is a reference,\n   the setter mutates in place) and WRONG for an abstract over a value type:\n   Haxe means `a.prop = 5` to become `a = set_prop(a, 5)`, **writing the result\n   back into the receiver lvalue**. Without write-back the value is computed and\n   thrown away. A silent wrong answer is worse than the E0100 it replaces —\n   this alone disqualified shipping it.\n\n2. **`haxe.Int64` regresses** — `low`/`high` start failing E0100 inside\n   `BytesBuffer` and `haxe.io.Output` (0 errors at HEAD, 6 with the change).\n   Int64 is `abstract Int64(__Int64)` whose accessors read the **same-named**\n   field on the underlying class: `inline function get_high() return this.high;`.\n   Inside an abstract, Haxe gives `this` the UNDERLYING type, so `this.high` is\n   the underlying's field — but rayzor resolves it to the abstract's own\n   property symbol, so the accessor routes back into the property it implements.\n\n   **Not fixable by the name-fallback guard.** I tried both: recording the owner\n   in `field_class_names` (the ownership filter needs BOTH owner and receiver\n   known, and the receiver inside the accessor is unknown), and a dedicated\n   `abstract_property_fields` set that the name-keyed fallback in\n   `mir/field/access.rs` skips. Neither helps, because the **exact-symbol**\n   lookup is what hits: `this.high` in the accessor resolves to the very same\n   SymbolId as the declared property.\n\n\n- **Abstract `this` semantics**: inside an abstract, `this.\u003cname\u003e` must resolve\n  to the underlying type's field, never to the abstract's own property. This is\n  the prerequisite — fix it before re-attempting the registration, or Int64\n  breaks again. Related: the implicit-`this` comment at\n  `tast/ast_lowering/decls/abstracts.rs` ~310 covers the read side of the same\n  confusion.\n- **Setter write-back** in `mir/field/lvalue.rs`: for a property whose owner is\n  an abstract, the setter's return value must be assigned back to the receiver\n  lvalue.\n\nProbes that work: `RAYZOR_E0100_DEBUG=1` at the failure site\n(`ir/mir/field/access.rs`), `RAYZOR_RESOLVE_TRACE=1` for name resolution.\n\n**TRAP that caught me twice this session** (see also\n[[bugs-named-function-declarations-bind-no-name]]): a fix that turns a clean\ncompile error into a silent wrong answer or a crash is a REGRESSION, not\npartial progress. Both times the first repro looked like a pass — a string\nconcat hid an unboxed return, and a read-only test hid a dropped write. Always\nexercise write AND read, and always run the control on the pre-change binary.\n\n- Index summary moved from MEMORY.md (2026-10-18): Abstract `(get,set)` properties unreachable (~8 cases). Getter half SOLVED; blocked on (1) setter needs VALUE WRITE-BACK `a = set_prop(a,5)`, (2) `this.high` in an accessor resolves to the abstract's OWN property ⇒ Int64 regresses. Attempt reverted.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_abstract_properties_need_setter_writeback` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}